  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 252 PM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nLow pressure over eastern Canada, which was the cause of yesterday's \nstorms, continues to move away from the Great Lakes region. Surface \nhigh pressure is now building in from the northwest, with a cooler \nand drier air mass filtering into Indiana. Dew points have slowly \nbeen falling through the day.\n\nAloft, troughing is located over the northeast with broad ridging \nover the Plains and intermountain west. Though northwesterly flow \npersists aloft, surface high pressure should keep things quiet this \nweek. Near normal temperatures are expected through Friday. From \nFriday onward, ensemble guidance shows a shortwave trough diving \nsoutheastward within the jet stream. Overall, the jet stream is \nrather weak and most guidance shows this system becoming cut off \nfrom the main flow.\n\nGuidance can struggle in situations with a cut off low embedded \nwithin a weak steering flow. Despite this, most guidance shows this \nsystem to some extent over the weekend. Timing and location vary, \nbut the signal is there. As such, we will carry chance to likely \nPoPs. A lot of details still need to be ironed out, however, given \nresidual variability within ensemble guidance.\n\nBeyond this weekend, ensemble guidance continues to diverge and \nshows no strong signal for ridging or troughing. The mean 500mb flow \nresides well to our north, however, so with the weekend upper low \ndissipating a trend towards warmer temperatures is favored for the \nfirst week of August.",
